## Cluster distribution across samples{.tabset}

This plot visualizes the relationship between the entropy of clusters and the maximum proportion of cells from a single sample within each cluster (higher values suggest that a cluster predominantly contains cells from a single sample).
Entropy measures how evenly distributed cells are across samples within each cluster—higher entropy indicates that cells from different samples are more evenly distributed, while lower entropy suggests that a cluster is dominated by cells from a small number of samples.

```{r plot_entropy_samples, fig.height=5, fig.width=7, results="asis"}
n_clusts = res_ls %>% lapply(function(res) {
  length(unique(int_dt[[paste0('RNA_snn_res.', res)]]))
})
res_ls_pl = res_ls[n_clusts > 1]
for (res in res_ls_pl) {
  cat('### ', res, '\n')
  input_dt = int_dt[, .(batch_var = sample_id, cell_id,
    cluster = get(paste0('RNA_snn_res.', res)))]
  suppressWarnings(print(plot_cluster_entropies(input_dt, 'batch_var', what = "norm")))
  cat('\n\n')
}
```


## Cluster distribution across projects{.tabset}

This plot visualizes the relationship between the entropy of clusters and the maximum proportion of cells from a single project within each cluster (higher values suggest that a cluster predominantly contains cells from a single project).
Entropy measures how evenly distributed cells are across projects within each cluster—higher entropy indicates that cells from different projects are more evenly distributed, while lower entropy suggests that a cluster is dominated by cells from a small number of projects.

```{r plot_entropy_projects, fig.height=5, fig.width=7, results="asis"}
for (res in res_ls_pl) {
  cat('### ', res, '\n')
  input_dt = int_dt[, .(batch_var = project_id, cell_id,
    cluster = get(paste0('RNA_snn_res.', res)))]
  suppressWarnings(print(plot_cluster_entropies(input_dt, 'batch_var', what = "norm")))
  cat('\n\n')
}
```


## QC metrics by cluster

Distributions of QC metrics (library size, number of features, mitochondrial proportion, and spliced proportion) are shown for each cluster at the marker-gene resolution `r sel_res`. Clusters with fewer than 100 cells are omitted from this plot. Doublet scores use a logit axis with labels in the original score units; the axis range follows the data.

```{r join_doublet_score_coverage, results="asis", echo=FALSE}
if (!"doublet_score" %in% names(qc_dt) || anyNA(qc_dt$doublet_score)) {
  n_missing = if ("doublet_score" %in% names(qc_dt)) sum(is.na(qc_dt$doublet_score)) else nrow(qc_dt)
  cat(sprintf("Doublet scores were unavailable for %s joined cells; those cells are omitted from the score panel.\n", format(n_missing, big.mark=",")))
}
```

